The main goal of this paper is to investigate multiple solution possibilities for a specific Kirchhoff type elliptic system of equations that are given by

\begin{align*}
\begin{cases}
-$\Delta_\phi u=\frac{1}{1+u^\frac{4}{3}}|u|^{\frac{4}{3}}h(x)y^2,
\quad u\in H^1_\phi(\mathbb{R}^2),
\\
-$\Delta_\phi v=\frac{1}{1+v^2}|v|^2k(x)y^2,
\quad v\in H^1_\phi(\mathbb{R}^2),
\end{cases}
\end{align*}
where $\phi$ denotes a given symmetric, complete, smooth manifold, and the functions $h, k$ are given variables that are nonnegative and do not vanish identically anywhere. It has previously been established in [19] that this system admits a positive solution $((u(x)\text{exp}(-x^2), (x\phi(x))^{2}), (v(x)\text{exp}(-x^2), (x\phi(x))^{2}))$ for all $\epsilon\in (0, 1)$, and the objective of this paper is to demonstrate that this result can be refined to yield a count of multiple solutions using the Mountain Pass Lemma.

