Between October 2019 and November 2020, the LAMOST performed a unique observational study, utilizing four $K$2 plates to investigate various celestial phenomena over a specific right ascension range. This study involved observing 1,617,119 unique targets, with a high optical magnitude limit of $18.85$ for its spectroscopic data. The LAMOST time-domain exploration of these targets yielded several initial findings, including: 
- A total of 1,607,079 targets with a single observation;
- 87,940 targets with multiple observations by LAMOST;
- 1,607,079 targets with a single spectroscopic observation, providing radial velocity data for the LAMOST $K$2  plates, with a significant number being radial velocity variables;
  - Among the single observation targets, approximately 11.7% of galaxies showed an emission line spectrum;
  - Among the single observation targets, approximately 0.4% of active galactic nuclei showed an emission line spectrum;
  - Among the single observation targets, approximately 0.07% of Quasars showed an emission line spectrum;
  - Among the multiple observation targets, approximately 0.4% of Quasars showed an emission line spectrum;
  - A total of 2,919 targets with a single spectroscopic observation were identified as white dwarfs;
